INIT = "duty_board/__init__.py"
DATA_PATH = "duty_board/academy_sales_pro_data.json"
SEED_PATH = "duty_board/academy_seed_sales_pro.py"
CHECK_ONLY = "--check" in sys.argv

L = lambda t, est, html: {"title": t, "est": est, "html": html}

LESSONS = [
L("Chapter 1 — The Customer master, field by field", "18", "<p>Every quotation, sales order, delivery note and invoice in ZhiftERP begins with a question: <i>who is this for?</i> The answer is a <b>Customer</b> record, and the quality of that record decides the quality of everything that follows. A well-built customer master means documents that default correctly, statements that reconcile, credit that is controlled, and reports that answer management's questions without a single spreadsheet. A carelessly built one means the opposite, for years. This chapter walks the record field by field.</p><p><b>Creating the record.</b> Navigate <b>Selling &gt; Customer &gt; New</b> (or press the plus from the Customer list). The form opens with the identity section on top. Before typing anything, understand the difference between the customer's <i>name</i> and the record's <i>ID</i>: what you type as Customer Name is the trading identity you'll see on documents; whether the system also uses it as the record's permanent ID, or issues a series number like CUST-00042 instead, is governed by <b>Customer Naming By</b> in Selling Settings (Chapter 7 and, in depth, Selling 8). For now: type the legal or trading name exactly, consistently cased — \"Alpha Pharmacy Ltd\", not \"alpha pharm\", not \"ALPHA PHARMACY (Mr Chidi's one)\".</p><p><b>Customer Type — Company or Individual.</b> Companies carry registration and tax identity and typically several contacts; individuals are natural persons. The choice affects which identity fields make sense (a Tax ID belongs to a company) and how you'll structure contacts. Retail chains, distributors, corporates: Company. A walk-in professional buying on account: Individual. When in doubt for B2B trade, it's Company.</p><p><b>Customer Group.</b> The first of the two great reporting dimensions. It answers <i>what kind</i> of customer this is — Retail, Pharmacy, Distributor, Corporate, Government — and every serious sales report can slice by it. Groups are covered as a design exercise in Chapter 2; at creation time the rule is simply: never leave the default unexamined. If your defaults chapter set \"All Customer Groups\" or a generic bucket, a lazily created customer disappears from segment analysis forever.</p><p><b>Territory.</b> The second great dimension: <i>where</i>. Lagos Mainland, Lagos Island, Abuja FCT, South-West. Chapter 3 designs the tree; the creation-time rule mirrors groups: choose deliberately. And two anti-patterns to refuse on sight: a territory named after a salesperson (people resign; Ikeja does not), and a territory set to a warehouse (fulfilment geography is not sales geography).</p><p><b>Default Price List.</b> Which set of selling rates this customer receives. When a quotation is raised for them, this list lands on the document and every row's rate is looked up from it (Selling 2 covers the machinery). Distributor customers point at the distributor list; retail at standard. Leave it blank and the global default from Selling Settings applies — acceptable for the common case, wrong for anyone on negotiated rates.</p><p><b>Default Currency.</b> The currency their documents are priced and their receivable is tracked in. For domestic trade: NGN. For your Ghanaian or export relationships: the trading currency. The diagnostic habit to build now: <i>when a customer's quotations keep defaulting to the wrong currency, the durable fix is this field on the master</i> — editing each quotation treats the symptom and guarantees the disease recurs on the next document.</p><p><b>Tax identity.</b> For Nigerian companies, record the <b>Tax ID (TIN)</b> — your FIRS e-invoicing pipeline (the NGE stack) and formal B2B invoicing both want it, and chasing TINs at invoice time is how billing stalls. Capture it at onboarding while the relationship is new and cooperative.</p><p><b>Accounts and terms (a preview).</b> The master also carries the customer's default <b>Payment Terms</b> (Net 30, advance-and-balance — inherited by invoices, as Selling 7 teaches), their receivable account override if your chart demands one, and the <b>Credit Limit</b> section that Chapter 6 treats end-to-end. The principle across all of them: <i>everything a document will need should already live on the master</i>, so document creation is selection, not invention.</p><p><b>The creation discipline.</b> A customer record is created once and transacted against for years. Create it deliberately: name, type, group, territory, currency, price list, TIN, terms — all set before the first quotation. \"Only a name, details later\" is the master-data equivalent of unhedged debt: later never comes, every document inherits blanks, and the dimensions you need at year-end were never captured. The master record is a contract with your future reports; sign it properly.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Customer Groups: designing the segmentation tree", "15", "<p>Customer Group is not a label; it is the backbone of every answer to \"how is each part of our market doing?\" This chapter treats it as what it is — a small piece of information architecture that deserves an hour of design before the first customer is created.</p><p><b>The mechanics.</b> Customer Groups live in a <b>tree</b> (Selling &gt; Customer Group, or the tree view). A group can be a <i>group node</i> (has children, holds no customers directly) or a <i>leaf</i> (customers attach here). Reports roll leaves up through their parents automatically: sales for \"Pharmacy — Independent\" and \"Pharmacy — Chain\" sum into \"Pharmacy\", which sums into \"Healthcare\", which sums into the root. You design the rollup once; every report inherits it forever.</p><p><b>Designing the tree — three rules.</b></p><ul><li><b>Rule 1: one question per tree.</b> The group tree answers <i>what kind of customer</i>. Resist the temptation to encode geography (that's Territory), size (consider a custom field or naming convention), or payment behaviour (that's credit data) into group names. \"Lagos Pharmacies (Big, Slow-paying)\" as a group is three dimensions crushed into one string — unreportable on any of them.</li><li><b>Rule 2: mirror how management actually talks.</b> If the MD asks \"how did Pharmacy do versus Distribution?\", those are your top branches. A tree that mirrors the org's mental model gets used; a taxonomically \"correct\" tree nobody recognises gets ignored and defaulted around.</li><li><b>Rule 3: leaves you can assign in two seconds.</b> Every real customer must land in exactly one obvious leaf. If your team debates where a customer goes, the tree is too clever. Six to fifteen leaves serves most Nigerian mid-market firms; thirty means you're encoding something that belongs elsewhere.</li></ul><p><b>A worked tree for a ZhiftERP retail-systems vendor:</b> All Customer Groups → <i>Retail</i> (→ Supermarket · Pharmacy · Specialty), <i>Distribution</i> (→ FMCG Distributor · Pharma Distributor), <i>Corporate &amp; Institutional</i>, <i>Government</i>. Eight leaves, every prospect obviously placeable, every board question answerable by rollup.</p><p><b>Groups as machinery, not just reporting.</b> The group is also a <i>condition</i> elsewhere in ZhiftERP: pricing rules can target a customer group (\"10% off for Distribution\", Selling 6), and defaults can hang from groups. That doubles the case for a clean tree — a pricing policy aimed at a mushy group hits mushy targets.</p><p><b>Changing your mind later.</b> Re-parenting a group or moving a customer between leaves is allowed and takes effect in reports immediately (they read current assignments). What you cannot recover is history never captured: customers dumped for a year into a generic default have a year of unsegmentable sales. Hence the Chapter 1 discipline — the tree is only as good as assignment at creation time.</p><p><b>The default group.</b> Selling Settings names a default group for hurried creation. Point it at your genuinely most common leaf — not at the root, and not at a \"Misc\" bucket that becomes a landfill. A weekly two-minute review of newly created customers (filter by creation date) catches misfiled ones while they're young.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Territories: geography as a reporting dimension", "14", "<p>Territory answers <i>where</i>. Like groups it is a tree, it rolls up in every report, and it is worth designing before data arrives — with one extra power groups don't have: territories can carry <b>targets</b>.</p><p><b>Designing for Nigeria.</b> The right granularity follows your commercial footprint, not the atlas. A Lagos-centric firm with national ambitions might use: All Territories → <i>Lagos</i> (→ Mainland · Island · Lagos West), <i>South-West (ex-Lagos)</i>, <i>Abuja &amp; North-Central</i>, <i>South-East</i>, <i>South-South</i>, <i>North</i>, <i>Export</i> (→ Ghana · Other West Africa). Twelve leaves. The tests: (1) every customer lands somewhere obvious; (2) each leaf is worth measuring on its own — if you'd never look at \"North-East\" alone, don't create it yet; split when the business arrives; (3) the top branches match how you'd staff regional responsibility, because territory performance and regional accountability should read off the same tree.</p><p><b>The two anti-patterns, again, with reasons.</b> A territory named after a salesperson welds an org-chart snapshot into your geography: when Tunde resigns, \"Tunde's Area\" becomes archaeology, and reassigning his customers rewrites history's meaning. People belong in the <b>Sales Person</b> tree (Selling 8), which exists precisely so geography and staffing can change independently. And a territory set to a warehouse confuses <i>where we sell</i> with <i>where we ship from</i> — a Mainland customer served from an Island warehouse is still Mainland revenue.</p><p><b>Targets on territories.</b> A territory can carry sales targets by item group and fiscal period. Set ₦120m for Lagos Mainland this year, and territory reports show actual against target with the tree rolling both up. This is the lightest-weight regional performance system you can run — no spreadsheets, no arguments about whose number is whose, because the number is computed from the same documents that raised the invoices.</p><p><b>Territory in the machinery.</b> Like groups, territories serve as conditions: pricing rules can target them (a promo for South-East only), and analytics slice by them natively (Sales Analytics grouped by Territory is your regional revenue map, Selling 6). Export as a branch matters here: separating Ghana under Export keeps cross-border pricing rules and FX-flavoured analysis clean.</p><p><b>Assignment and hygiene.</b> Every customer gets exactly one territory at creation (Chapter 1). For chains, the territory of the <i>customer</i> is where the relationship is managed — usually the head office — while individual branch geography lives on the shipping addresses (Chapter 5); don't fragment one chain across territories unless you genuinely manage the regions as separate relationships. Same weekly hygiene habit as groups: review new customers, catch misfiles early.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — Contacts: the people layer", "13", "<p>A Customer is a company; you cannot phone a company. The people you actually deal with — the procurement officer, the pharmacist-owner, the accounts payable clerk — live as <b>Contact</b> records linked to the customer. Modelling them properly is the difference between institutional relationships and relationships that resign when one staff member (theirs or yours) does.</p><p><b>What a Contact carries.</b> First and last name, designation (\"Head of Procurement\"), one or more email addresses, one or more phone numbers (each list with its own primary), and the link to the customer (a contact can even serve multiple parties — a consultant procuring for two sister companies). Create contacts from the customer's dashboard (the linked Contacts section) so the link is born correct, or from the Contact list directly.</p><p><b>The placement rule.</b> When a buyer gives you a phone number, it goes on a Contact. Not appended to the customer name (\"Alpha Pharmacy — call Ngozi 0803…\" is a real record in many bad implementations), not buried in a quotation's remarks (unfindable in a month), not in a private phonebook (gone when your rep leaves). The Contact record is searchable, appears on documents, syncs to communications, and — critically — belongs to the <i>company's</i> relationship with the customer, not to any individual rep's.</p><p><b>Primary contact.</b> Exactly one contact per customer should carry the primary flag. Documents pull the <b>primary contact by default</b> — not the newest contact, not the first alphabetically, not whoever was used last. So the operational meaning of \"primary\" is: <i>who should a document address when nobody chooses?</i> Usually the commercial decision-maker for quotations. You can always switch a given document to another of the customer's contacts; primary just sets the sane starting point.</p><p><b>Multiple contacts, deliberately.</b> A healthy B2B customer record carries at least three people: the commercial contact (quotations, negotiations), the operational contact (deliveries — the store manager who receives goods), and the finance contact (invoices, statements, the person you chase at collections time, Selling 7). Capturing all three at onboarding costs five minutes and removes the single most common friction in fulfilment and collections: \"who do we even talk to about this?\"</p><p><b>Lifecycle.</b> People move. When the procurement officer changes, you edit or replace the contact and re-flag primary — the customer, its history, its balances are untouched. That separation (company-level customer, person-level contacts) is exactly why the layer exists; firms that encode people into customer names get to rename their history every time someone resigns.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Addresses & the chain-modelling walkthrough", "16", "<p>Addresses answer <i>where things happen</i>: where invoices are addressed, where goods land. Like contacts they are separate records linked to the customer — one company, many places — and they carry the single most consequential modelling decision in Nigerian retail implementations: <b>how to represent a chain</b>.</p><p><b>What an Address carries.</b> Title, address lines, city, state, country; a type — <b>Billing</b>, <b>Shipping</b>, or both; the link to its customer; and a primary flag per role. Create from the customer's dashboard so linkage is automatic.</p><p><b>Billing vs shipping.</b> The billing address is the invoice's legal face — head office, where the TIN lives. Shipping addresses are delivery reality — stores, warehouses, branches. One customer routinely has one billing address and many shipping addresses, and documents let you pick: the invoice wears billing; each delivery note wears the branch it's actually going to.</p><p><b>Primary flags.</b> As with contacts: documents pull the <b>primary address by default</b>. When a quotation shows the wrong address, the fix is almost always on the address records — the wrong one is flagged primary, or the right one was never created — not on the quotation. Diagnose at the master; the documents will follow.</p><p><b>THE CHAIN WALKTHROUGH.</b> Your customer is a pharmacy chain: head office in Ikeja, 30 branches across Lagos and Ibadan, one procurement department, one account to settle. The correct model, end to end:</p><ol><li><b>One Customer record</b> — \"MediPlus Pharmacies Ltd\". One, not thirty. The customer is the <i>commercial relationship</i>: one credit limit governing total exposure, one receivable balance, one statement, one negotiated price list, one history. Thirty customer records would shatter every one of those into thirty fragments — credit control becomes fiction (each \"customer\" under its limit while the chain owes triple your comfort), statements become a monthly reconciliation war, and \"how much did MediPlus buy this year?\" becomes a report nobody can run.</li><li><b>One billing address</b> — head office, primary billing. Every invoice addresses Ikeja, where accounts payable lives.</li><li><b>Thirty shipping addresses</b> — \"MediPlus — Surulere\", \"MediPlus — Yaba\", … \"MediPlus — Bodija\". Named so pickers and drivers recognise them instantly. Each delivery note selects its branch; the waybill prints the right street; and delivered-to history per branch is queryable forever.</li><li><b>Contacts per role</b> (Chapter 4) — procurement at HQ (primary), finance at HQ, and optionally key store managers attached as operational contacts.</li><li><b>What NOT to do</b> — addresses retyped per order (typos become failed deliveries and history becomes text nobody can group), or a hybrid \"main branches as customers, small ones as addresses\" (the worst of both: fragmented finance <i>and</i> inconsistent shipping data).</li></ol><p><b>The exception that proves the rule.</b> Model branches as separate customers only when they genuinely are separate commercial relationships — franchisees who buy and pay independently, each with its own credit standing. Then each franchisee IS a customer (perhaps grouped under a \"MediPlus Franchise\" customer group for rollup). The test is always: <i>who owes the money?</i> One payer, one customer.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Credit limits & the credit-control workflow", "16", "<p>Every credit sale is a loan wearing a delivery note. This chapter is the end-to-end workflow that keeps those loans deliberate: the limit, the block, the controller, and the review.</p><p><b>What the limit is — precisely.</b> The <b>Credit Limit</b>, set in the customer's Credit Limits section (settable per company if you run several), caps the customer's <b>unpaid outstanding exposure</b>: their open invoices plus committed-but-unbilled orders, now. It is not lifetime purchases (a decade-old customer hasn't \"used up\" anything by volume), not a count of orders, and not a discount ceiling. Exposure — what you'd lose if they vanished tonight.</p><p><b>Setting the number.</b> New customer, no history: start conservative — one typical order's value, or trade-reference-informed. Established customer: their payment behaviour is your underwriting; a customer who clears ₦8m monthly like clockwork can carry ₦10–15m. The limit is a policy dial, reviewed (see below), not a one-time guess.</p><p><b>The block.</b> When a new Sales Order or invoice would push outstanding past the limit, ZhiftERP <b>blocks submission</b> with a credit-limit message. Understand the timing philosophically: the block fires at the moment of maximum temptation — the deal is closing, the rep wants the number, the customer is asking nicely. That is exactly when policy must be mechanical, because \"just this once\" evaluated by the person carrying the sales target is not evaluation.</p><p><b>The bypass — the Credit Controller.</b> Exceptions must be possible (a strategic customer mid-payment-run deserves flexibility) but must be <i>owned</i>. Only a user holding the <b>Credit Controller</b> role can release a blocked document — per document, deliberately, their name on the action. Not any System Manager by default, not the salesperson who raised it, and not \"nobody until they pay\" (that's not a policy, it's an outage). Organisationally: give the role to finance, never to sales — the person who wants the deal must not be the person who waives the risk. In an 11-person firm that's the accountant or the MD; the point is the separation, not the headcount.</p><p><b>The bypass conversation, scripted.</b> Rep hits the block → rep messages the controller with three facts: how much over, what's the customer's ageing (Selling 7's AR report), and when payment is expected → controller decides: release this document, raise the limit properly, or hold the order (Selling 4) until money lands. Sixty seconds, recorded in the system's own trail. What the workflow prevents is the alternative that kills firms slowly: goods shipping on charm while finance discovers the exposure at month-end.</p><p><b>The review cadence.</b> Quarterly, run the AR ageing next to the limits: customers paying flawlessly at 80% utilisation are candidates for raises (don't strangle good growth); customers living in the 61-90 bucket get cuts or cash-only status. Limits that never move are limits nobody believes in.</p><p><b>The commercial truth to teach every rep:</b> the difference between a sales team that grows revenue and one that grows bad debt is rarely closing skill — it is whether credit policy executes automatically at the point of entry or gets \"reviewed\" by humans after the goods have left. The block is not the system doubting you; it is the system doing the one job charm cannot.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Selling Settings for foundations", "12", "<p>Selling Settings (type it in the awesome bar) is one screen of switches where selling policy becomes system behaviour. Selling 8 walks every switch as the advanced treatment; a foundations trainee needs to command the following subset now, because they shape the documents you'll make from Chapter 1's customers.</p><p><b>Customer Naming By.</b> <i>Customer Name</i> makes the typed name the record's permanent ID; <i>Naming Series</i> issues CUST-00042-style IDs with the name as display label. Preview of the Selling 8 decision rule: portfolios with duplicate or near-duplicate names — chains, common Nigerian trading names — need the series, because IDs must be unambiguous forever even when names aren't. What matters at foundations level: know that this is where record identity is decided, and that it's a day-one decision (changing naming schemes mid-life is migration surgery).</p><p><b>The defaults trio.</b> Default <b>Customer Group</b>, default <b>Territory</b>, default <b>Selling Price List</b>. These pre-fill new customers and documents when nobody chooses. Set each to your genuinely commonest case — hurried data entry then lands <i>correctly</i> instead of blank. And connect this to Chapters 2–3's hygiene: defaults are a safety net, not a filing system; the weekly new-customer review catches what fell into the net.</p><p><b>Allow Item to be Added Multiple Times in a Transaction.</b> Off: the same item cannot appear on two rows of one order (duplicate protection). On: it can — the legitimate case being the same item at two rates on one document, like 100 units at list plus 20 promotional units at zero, as two visible rows rather than a doctored average rate. This switch lives HERE — not on the item master, not on the price list, not in a print format — and knowing its address is a straight exam fact and a straight support fact.</p><p><b>Maintain Same Rate Throughout Sales Cycle.</b> The rate agreed on the Sales Order cannot silently differ on the Delivery Note or Sales Invoice made from it. What it defends against is precisely <b>rate drift between the documents of one sale</b> — the ₦850 the customer signed becoming ₦880 at invoice because someone \"tidied\" it. It is not currency management (Selling 2's conversion machinery), not discount control (Selling 6), not tax recalculation (templates). Its companion <i>action</i> setting — hard <b>Stop</b> versus <b>Warning</b> — is the Selling 8 discussion; foundations level: know the switch, know what drift means, know why a customer who signs one number and is billed another becomes a former customer.</p><p><b>The consultant's reflex to build now:</b> when a client asks \"can the system stop staff doing X?\", open this screen before you say anything. Most selling-policy questions are already switches; the expensive consultant is the one who writes a customisation for a checkbox.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: onboarding a 30-branch pharmacy chain", "18", "<p>Theory becomes competence in the doing. This chapter onboards a realistic customer end-to-end using everything in this module. Read it actively — every step names the chapter that taught it.</p><p><b>The client.</b> MediPlus Pharmacies Ltd: head office Ikeja, 30 branches (24 Lagos, 6 Ibadan), central procurement, central payment, negotiated distributor-grade pricing, requesting 45-day terms. Your firm has agreed to supply.</p><p><b>Step 1 — The customer record (Ch. 1).</b> Selling &gt; Customer &gt; New. Name: <i>MediPlus Pharmacies Ltd</i> (their CAC name). Type: Company. Customer Group: <i>Retail → Pharmacy — Chain</i> (Ch. 2's tree earning its keep: chain pharmacies report separately from independents). Territory: <i>Lagos → Mainland</i> — the relationship is managed from Ikeja; branch geography will live on addresses, and we do NOT fragment one payer across territories (Ch. 3, Ch. 5). Default Currency: NGN. Default Price List: <i>Distributor NGN</i> — their negotiated tier, so every quotation prices correctly without anyone remembering the deal (Ch. 1; machinery in Selling 2). Tax ID: their TIN, captured now while onboarding goodwill is high.</p><p><b>Step 2 — Payment terms.</b> They asked 45 days; management approved. Set default Payment Terms on the customer to the Net-45 template — every invoice will inherit it (Selling 7) and nobody improvises terms at billing time.</p><p><b>Step 3 — Contacts (Ch. 4).</b> Three people, created from the customer's dashboard: <i>Ngozi E.</i>, Head of Procurement — <b>primary</b> (quotations address her); <i>Ibrahim S.</i>, Accounts Payable (statements and collections address him — future-you at Selling 7 says thanks); <i>Kemi A.</i>, Regional Ops (delivery escalations). Five minutes now; the \"who do we even call?\" friction never exists.</p><p><b>Step 4 — Addresses (Ch. 5).</b> One billing address: head office, Ikeja — primary billing; every invoice wears it. Thirty shipping addresses, one per branch, named for humans: \"MediPlus — Surulere\", … \"MediPlus — Bodija\". (Thirty entries is an import job, not an afternoon of typing — the same import discipline Selling 2 teaches for prices.) Flag the highest-volume branch primary shipping; every delivery note picks its actual branch.</p><p><b>Step 5 — Credit (Ch. 6).</b> Projected offtake ₦6m/month on 45-day terms means roughly two months' exposure in steady state: recommend a ₦15m limit, approved by finance, entered on the customer. Confirm the Credit Controller role sits with the accountant, not the sales lead. Diarise the first review for the end of quarter one: their actual payment behaviour will tell you whether ₦15m was generous or tight.</p><p><b>Step 6 — Verify the defaults end-to-end.</b> Raise a draft test quotation for MediPlus (delete after): price list shows Distributor NGN ✓; currency NGN ✓; contact Ngozi ✓; billing address Ikeja ✓; a delivery-note address picker shows all thirty branches ✓. Ten minutes of verification beats a first live order that ships to the wrong city at retail rates.</p><p><b>Step 7 — The one-paragraph handover.</b> In the client room / CRM: \"MediPlus configured: 1 customer, 30 shipping addresses (import file attached), Distributor NGN pricing, Net-45, ₦15m limit (review 30 Sept), contacts: Ngozi (commercial), Ibrahim (finance), Kemi (ops).\" The next consultant — or you in six months — reconstructs the whole setup from four lines.</p><p><b>What this setup buys, permanently:</b> one statement Ibrahim can reconcile; one limit that actually caps the chain; branch-level delivery history; segment-true reporting (Pharmacy—Chain, Lagos Mainland); pricing that defaults right on every future document. Total work: under two hours, most of it the address import. Every hour skipped here is repaid weekly, with interest, forever.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & support patterns", "14", "<p>Every implementation team accumulates the same scar tissue. This chapter hands you the scars pre-earned: the foundations mistakes you will meet in the field, how they present as support tickets, and the fixes — so you recognise the disease from the symptom in one move.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — \"The quotation shows the wrong address/contact.\"</b> Disease: primary flags (Ch. 4-5) — the wrong record is flagged primary, or the right record was never created and the only one wins by default. Fix at the master: create/flag correctly; the documents follow. Anti-fix: editing the document, which cures one quotation and leaves every future one wrong.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — \"Wrong currency/prices keep appearing for this customer.\"</b> Disease: Default Currency or Default Price List on the master (Ch. 1). The document is faithfully inheriting a wrong default. Fix the field; stop treating documents.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — \"We can't tell how much the chain owes us\" / statements chaos.</b> Disease: the chain was modelled as many customers (Ch. 5's cardinal sin). Each fragment is individually under-limit and individually statement-ed while the real payer's exposure is invisible. Fix: consolidate to one customer with shipping addresses — a data-migration exercise (re-pointing open documents and balances) that gets harder every month it waits, which is why the walkthrough exists.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — \"Reports say most of our sales are in 'All Customer Groups'.\"</b> Disease: creation-time laziness plus a root-level default (Ch. 2, Ch. 7). Everyone was filed in the landfill. Fix forward: set a real default leaf, institute the weekly new-customer review. Fix backward: a one-time regrouping exercise — tedious, and the reports only speak from the day assignments are true.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — \"Sales by region is meaningless / territories named after ex-staff.\"</b> Disease: people encoded into geography (Ch. 3). Fix: rebuild territories as geography, move staffing to the Sales Person tree (Selling 8), reassign customers once.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — \"The system blocked my order and the client is waiting!\"</b> Not a disease — the credit block working (Ch. 6). The support answer is the workflow, not a workaround: check exposure and ageing, route to the Credit Controller, release / raise / hold deliberately. The ticket to ESCALATE is its inverse: \"can you give my user the bypass so this stops happening\" — that's a request to disable credit control, and it goes to management, not to a permissions tweak.</p><p><b>Pattern 7 — \"Duplicate customers everywhere.\"</b> Disease: name-based creation without discipline (\"Alpha Pharmacy\", \"Alpha Pharmacy Ltd\", \"ALPHA PHARM\") — each with partial history. Prevention: naming-series IDs (Ch. 7 / Selling 8) plus a search-before-create habit. Cure: merge/re-point, then prevention — duplicates recreate themselves wherever the habit that made them survives.</p><p><b>The meta-pattern.</b> Read the seven again: every single one is a master-data disease presenting as a document symptom. That is the deepest lesson of this module — <i>documents are downstream of masters</i>. The consultant who fixes masters closes tickets permanently; the one who fixes documents subscribes to them. When a selling support ticket arrives, your first question is now reflex: <b>which master record is lying?</b></p>"),
]

